Architectural Flow 
1. Architectural Overview
The Integrated GRC Risk Governance System represents a vertically structured architecture that connects external threat intelligence, operational control context, and governance oversight into a coherent decision support mechanism.
The system operates through two complementary flows:
•	Top-Down Configuration Flow->Governance defines objectives and risk appetite.
•	Bottom-Up Risk Propagation Flow->External vulnerability signals are ingested, evaluated, contextualised, and escalated to governance stakeholders.
The architecture does not perform vulnerability scanning, remediation, or compliance verification. Instead, it functions as a governance-layer visibility and interpretation system designed to enhance decision readiness.

2. System Boundary
The outer boundary labelled Integrated GRC–Risk Governance System (COBIT–NIST) represents the artefact developed in this project.
It includes:
•	Governance configuration logic
•	Control mapping structure
•	Data ingestion
•	Risk evaluation
•	Risk register generation
•	Dashboard visualisation
It excludes:
•	Asset-level scanning
•	Patch deployment
•	Incident response execution
This boundary clearly establishes the artefact as a governance decision-support mechanism.

3. Public Risk Intelligence Sources
Public Risk Intelligence Sources (NVD API / Static Datasets)
This component represents external vulnerability intelligence, including CVE records and CVSS severity scores sourced from publicly available datasets.
Arrow Direction:
From Public Risk Intelligence Sources->Data Ingestion Layer
Meaning:
Risk signals originate externally. The system does not generate threats; it receives and processes them. This is the starting point of the bottom-up risk propagation flow.

4. Governance Configuration Module
Governance Configuration Module (COBIT Objectives & Risk Appetite)
This module defines:
•	Governance objectives (e.g., risk optimisation, risk management, security services oversight)
•	Risk appetite thresholds (High / Medium / Low boundaries)
Arrow Directions:
Governance Configuration->Control Mapping Engine
Governance Configuration->Governance Stakeholders
Meaning:
Governance intent is human-defined. Risk appetite and oversight priorities are established before runtime evaluation begins. This represents the top-down configuration layer of the system.

5. Control Mapping Engine
Control Mapping Engine (COBIT–NIST Control Mapping)
This module contains the predefined crosswalk linking governance objectives to operational control domains.
It provides structural alignment between:
•	Governance objectives (strategic oversight)
•	NIST control domains (operational context)
Arrow Direction:
Control Mapping Engine->Risk Evaluation and Register
Meaning:
The mapping does not assign severity. It provides interpretive context that structures how risk signals will later be understood at governance level.

6. Data Ingestion Layer
Data Ingestion Layer (Public Risk & Vulnerability Data)
This module:
•	Retrieves CVE data
•	Extracts CVSS base scores
•	Normalises vulnerability information
Arrow Directions:
Public Risk Intelligence->Data Ingestion Layer
Data Ingestion Layer->Risk Evaluation Engine
Meaning:
This is the beginning of bottom-up runtime processing. Raw vulnerability intelligence is transformed into structured input for risk evaluation.

7. Risk Evaluation Engine
Risk Evaluation Engine (Rule-Based Risk Scoring & Assessment)
This module:
•	Applies predefined CVSS thresholds
•	Translates CVSS into High / Medium / Low
•	Aligns classification with defined risk appetite
Arrow Direction:
Risk Evaluation Engine->Integrated Governance Risk Register
Meaning:
This is the core translation layer. Technical severity is converted into governance ready language. This is where external threat signals become appetite aligned risk categories.

8. Integrated Governance Risk Register
Integrated Governance Risk Register (Explainable, Traceable)
This component:
•	Stores CVE ID
•	Records CVSS score
•	Assigns governance-level risk category
•	Includes governance-control crosswalk context
Arrow Directions:
Risk Register->Integration & Visualisation Layer
Risk Register->Governance Stakeholders
Meaning:
The register provides structured traceability. It enables accountability, documentation, and explainable governance insight.

9. Integration & Visualisation Layer
Integration & Visualisation Layer (GRC Dashboard & Governance Insights)
This module:
•	Aggregates risk levels
•	Displays High/Medium/Low distribution
•	Presents interactive governance-ready dashboard outputs
Arrow Direction:
Dashboard->Governance Stakeholders
Meaning:
Data is transformed into decision-ready insight. This is where evaluation becomes communicable governance information.

10. Governance Stakeholders
Box: Governance Stakeholders (Board / Risk Committee)
This represents:
•	Human oversight bodies
•	Risk appetite setters
•	Decision-makers
Arrow Directions:
Governance Stakeholders->Governance Configuration Module (intent and appetite)
Dashboard->Governance Stakeholders (insight and escalation)
Meaning:
Governance remains human-in-the-loop. The system informs but does not automate decisions.

11. End-to-End Flow Sequence
1.	Governance objectives and risk appetite are defined (top-down configuration).
2.	External CVE data is retrieved from public sources.
3.	CVSS scores are extracted and normalised.
4.	CVSS is translated into High/Medium/Low using rule-based thresholds.
5.	Risk categories are contextualised using the COBIT–NIST crosswalk.
6.	An integrated governance risk register is generated.
7.	Risk distribution and structured insights are displayed via dashboard.
8.	Governance stakeholders interpret the outputs and make informed decisions.

12. What the Architecture Achieves
This architecture achieves:
•	Vertical integration across governance, control, and threat layers
•	Structured traceability from external vulnerability signals to governance oversight
•	Risk appetite operationalisation
•	Clear separation between evaluation and execution
•	Human-in-the-loop decision support
•	Reproducible and ethically grounded design using public data

13. Architectural Contribution
The architecture operationalises integrated GRC principles by enabling bottom-up propagation of external threat intelligence through structured control context into governance oversight, while preserving top-down human-defined risk intent.

